# Tracking-index discipline

`docs/design/IMPLEMENTATION_INDEX.md` is the single disambiguation
layer for the project's identifier families (Phase N, Stage N,
Bond-PR N, SP-N, SP-TN, PR-E-N, GF-N, F-N, …) and the verified map of
what is live, built-but-unwired, or missing.

This rule exists because the schemes grew independently and collided:
`2a–2d` meant different things in `WALLET_REWRITE_PLAN.md` and
`ARCHIVAL_BOND_PR2_CHAIN.md`, and `2c-2b` was reused *within* one
scheme. Reconstructing that from plan prose cost a full verification
pass. The index makes the reconstruction unnecessary; this rule keeps
the index true.

## Fail closed

If the index is missing or unreadable at
`docs/design/IMPLEMENTATION_INDEX.md` (a branch predating it, a bad
rebase), do **not** improvise: mint no new identifier families and
start no Phase 3+ / Stage 3+ implementation work. Halt and flag the
missing index to the user.

This rule names index sections semantically: the **identifier-family
registry** (§2 at time of writing), the **live / built-unwired /
missing inventory** (§4–§5), and the **known-stale-statuses ledger**
(§6). Keeping those sections' meanings stable is part of the index's
own contract; a restructuring that moves them updates this rule in
the same PR.

## Rules

1. **New identifier families register at birth.** A PR that mints a
   new prefix series in a plan doc adds a registry row **in the same
   PR**. Before minting, check the registry for collision. A new
   family is **ambiguous** iff a token it will mint could parse as a
   token of an existing family — mechanically: the family's
   alphabetic prefix up to the first digit (`SP-`, `SP-T`, `GF-`,
   `FA-`) must be unique among registered families. `SP-N` and
   `SP-TN` legitimately coexist (token sets cannot collide), as do
   `F-N` and `FA-N`. Never re-issue bare `2a`-style tokens.

   Concurrent PRs adding registry rows will merge-conflict on the
   table — that is the mechanism working. Conflict resolution
   **re-runs the uniqueness check against the merged table**; never
   resolve by textually keeping both hunks unexamined.

2. **No bare phase/track tokens going forward.** New prose, new
   commit messages, and new PR titles write `Phase 2b` /
   `Bond-PR 2b` / `Stage 3` — never a bare `2b` whose scheme the
   reader must infer (this also makes `git log --grep` archaeology
   work). Existing branch names, commit messages, and PR titles are
   immutable history and exempt. (Pinned 2026-06-19,
   `ARCHIVAL_BOND_PR2_CHAIN.md` §0.)

3. **Statuses are dated and verified — two verification classes.**
   - **Code-anchored:** items with a code artifact verify against
     landed code (`git grep` on `dev`), never against another doc's
     prose. Where a plan doc and code disagree, **code wins**; record
     the disagreement in the known-stale-statuses ledger and fix the
     plan doc in a scoped follow-up, not as drive-by churn.
   - **Decision-anchored:** items with no code artifact (round
     closures, wire freezes, contract docs) cite the commit hash or
     PR number of the doc edit that closed them. The closing commit
     wins where no code exists.

   Status edits are additive `UPDATE YYYY-MM-DD:` entries, and every
   row **leads with its current status + as-of date**, with the
   `UPDATE` chain beneath — a reader gets the answer without parsing
   history (row format spec lives in the index itself).

4. **Landing a tracked item updates the index.** The rule-91 doc
   task for any PR that lands, wires, or unblocks an inventory item
   includes the index row. Exception: when a PR deliberately avoids
   doc churn (e.g. conflict avoidance with an in-flight PR), the
   deferral takes rule-21 shape — the PR description names a
   **concrete carrier**: a PR/issue number or a landing condition
   ("carried by the 2c-2b partition PR"), never "a follow-up will
   handle it."

5. **Phase 3+ / Stage 3+ work starts registered.** From
   `WALLET_REWRITE_PLAN.md` Phase 3 onward (and for all remaining
   Stage-3+ implementation), a work item without an index row does
   not begin implementation. The row costs one table line at design
   time; the alternative is re-running the archaeology this rule was
   written to end. Pre-existing Phase-2-era items are grandfathered:
   they gain rows when a PR **changes their status** (lands, wires,
   supersedes, or closes them) — a passing textual mention does not
   trigger the row.

## Enforcement

Review-borne and agent halt-condition **only** — no CI backstop
exists today; nothing in `.github/workflows/` checks the registry or
lints doc tokens. A minimal lint is feasible (registry-prefix
uniqueness; diff-scoped bare-token detection in `docs/design/*.md`)
and is carried by the stale-status fix pass that the index's
known-stale-statuses ledger already mandates. Until that lands, do
not treat these rules as automatically enforced.

## Worked example

Minting a hypothetical emission-audit series: check the registry
(`EA-` prefix unused, cannot parse as any existing family's tokens),
add `**EA-1…EA-N** | emission-audit findings | REWARD_EMISSION_LEG.md`
to the registry in the PR that introduces `EA-1`, and write `EA-1`
(never bare `1`) in all prose. A later PR landing `EA-3` updates the
row: `Landed (as of 2026-08-01)` up front, then
`UPDATE 2026-08-01: EA-3 landed #NNN (verified: git grep
emission_audit on dev → emission/audit.rs)`. A design-only closure
cites its closing edit instead: `(decision-anchored: closed by
#NNN / abc1234)`.

